af(4,'bm61-market-orientation','4.1','sl','Market and product orientation','market orientation','An approach beginning with understanding customers’ needs and preferences / then designing the product and marketing activities to satisfy those needs.','Explain one benefit and one limitation of a more market-oriented approach at {name}.',[
('Qxa Audio','Qxa’s engineers have designed technically advanced speakers. Interviews suggest customers mainly want simple controls and easier repair, but engineers want to add more settings.', ['Customer research can reveal valued benefits.','Simple controls and repair access may matter more than extra settings.','Qxa can redirect design toward features customers are more likely to buy.'],['Research can constrain innovation if treated mechanically.','Customers may describe existing preferences rather than imagine new applications.','Qxa should test useful technical ideas instead of abandoning every feature not mentioned in interviews.']),
('Rxa Foods','Rxa makes high-protein snacks based on its founder’s recipe. Customer feedback requests smaller portions and fewer artificial flavours; the founder believes the original recipe is unique.', ['A market-oriented approach can improve product fit.','Smaller portions respond to feedback rather than only the founder’s preferences.','Rxa may attract customers who currently reject the portion size.'],['Research and adaptation can cost money.','Changing portions and ingredients may need new packaging and testing.','Rxa must check whether the extra demand will justify these costs.']),
('Sxa Tutors','Sxa offers the same long course to every student. Parents request shorter targeted modules; teachers worry that fragmented learning will weaken progression.', ['Customer understanding can identify unmet needs.','Targeted modules may suit families unable to commit to a long course.','Sxa could reach an additional group without assuming all learners want one format.'],['Customer preferences may conflict with educational outcomes.','Very short modules may not build prerequisite skills.','Sxa should combine parent research with teaching expertise when designing the offer.']),
('Txa Mobility','Txa builds rugged scooters. Users praise durability but request lighter frames, while engineers warn that very light materials increase cost.', ['Market orientation highlights a usability problem.','Lighter frames could make scooters easier for users to carry.','Txa can assess a feature linked directly to customer convenience.'],['Satisfying every expressed need may be uneconomic.','Lighter materials increase the cost of retaining durability.','Txa must research willingness to pay rather than assume all users will fund the change.'])])
for name,sector,own1,market1,own2,market2 in [('Uxa Seeds','seed packets',180000,1200000,231000,1400000),('Vxa Books','e-books',96000,800000,108000,1000000),('Wxa Fitness','memberships',42000,280000,60000,300000),('Xxa Lamps','lamps',75000,500000,84000,600000)]:
    share1=own1/market1*100;share2=own2/market2*100;growth=(market2-market1)/market1*100
    items=[calc('a','Calculate Year 2 market share by volume.',f'{own2}/{market2}×100.',f'{share2:.2f}%.'),calc('b','Calculate market growth by volume from Year 1 to Year 2.',f'({market2}−{market1})/{market1}×100.',f'{growth:.2f}%.'),explain('c','Explain why an increase in this business’s sales volume does not necessarily mean a higher market share.',['Market share compares the business with the whole market.',f'Its share changes from {share1:.2f}% to {share2:.2f}%, even though its own volume rises.','If the overall market grows faster than the business, the business’s share falls.','Absolute sales growth and relative market position must therefore be distinguished.'])]
    add(4,'bm62-market-volume','4.1','sl',f'{name}: share and market growth','Market share and growth',f'{name} sells {sector}. All figures count the same unit in the same national market:\n\n| Volume | Year 1 | Year 2 |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| {name} sales | {own1:,} | {own2:,} |\n| Total market, including {name} | {market1:,} | {market2:,} |\n\nUse volume, not revenue. Show workings and give percentages to two decimal places.',items,kind='data-response',check=f'MarketshareY1={share1};Y2={share2};marketgrowth={growth}; totalmarket includes own sales; denominator not competingfirms only.')
af(4,'bm63-segmentation','4.2','sl','Segmentation and targeting','market segmentation','Dividing a market into groups of customers with shared characteristics or needs / to enable a business to target groups with an appropriate marketing mix.','Explain two ways {name} could segment and target the market using the case information.',[
('Yxa Travel','Yxa sells rail holidays. Retired travellers request weekday departures and step-free access; younger workers request short weekend trips and online booking.', ['Life stage can define a segment.','Retired customers value weekday departures and accessible travel.','Yxa can target them with suitable itineraries rather than a single standard weekend package.'],['Lifestyle or work pattern can define another segment.','Younger workers have limited time and prefer short weekends.','Yxa can design quick online-booked trips to match those constraints.']),
('Zxa Foods','Zxa sells prepared lunches. Office customers want fast collection near workplaces; sports-club customers ask for protein-rich meals and evening delivery.', ['Geographic or usage context can define office customers.','They need convenient collection near work.','Zxa can target nearby offices with a lunchtime collection offer.'],['Benefits sought can define sports customers.','They value protein content and evening availability.','Zxa can use a distinct meal specification and delivery schedule for that segment.']),
('Aya Bikes','Aya sells bicycles. Daily commuters seek weather resistance and repair access, while occasional leisure riders prioritize low purchase prices.', ['Usage can define a commuter segment.','Daily riders place high value on durability and repair access.','Aya can target them with a service-supported commuting bicycle.'],['Frequency of use and price sensitivity can define leisure riders.','Occasional users may not value expensive daily-use features.','Aya can offer a simpler lower-priced model without assuming the commuter mix suits both groups.']),
('Bya Apps','Bya offers language learning. Some users need short travel phrases; others need professional vocabulary and formal progress records for work.', ['Purpose of use can define travellers.','They need practical phrases for a short trip.','Bya can target them with brief situation-based modules.'],['Occupational needs can define professional users.','They value specialist vocabulary and evidence of progress.','Bya can target employers with structured courses and reporting.'])])
for name,sector,points in [('Cya Bags','day bags',[('A',8,8),('B',3,4),('C',7,3),('New',5,8)]),('Dya Hotels','hotels',[('A',9,8),('B',3,3),('C',7,4),('New',5,7)]),('Eya Meals','meal subscriptions',[('A',8,9),('B',2,3),('C',7,3),('New',4,8)]),('Fya Audio','headphones',[('A',9,9),('B',3,2),('C',8,4),('New',5,8)])]:
    body='<path d="M70 250 L690 250 M70 250 L70 30" fill="none" stroke="#172554"/>'+svg_text(290,292,'Perceived price: low → high')+svg_text(10,25,'Perceived quality: low → high',13)
    for label,x,y in points:body+=f'<circle cx="{70+60*x}" cy="{250-20*y}" r="5" fill="#2563eb"/>'+svg_text(78+60*x,245-20*y,label)
    items=[define('a','position map','A visual representation of customers’ perceptions of competing products / plotted against selected attributes such as perceived price and quality.'),explain('b','Explain the intended positioning of New relative to A and B.',['New is perceived as cheaper than A.','Its similar high perceived quality suggests a value-for-money position relative to A.','New is perceived as higher-quality and higher-priced than B.','It therefore does not compete solely on the lowest price against B.']),explain('c','Explain one limitation of using this map alone to decide whether to launch New.',['Perceptions do not directly measure purchase intentions or segment size.','An apparently empty position may reflect low demand rather than an opportunity.','The two attributes omit other benefits such as convenience, durability or service.','Research on target customers and costs is required before treating the position as commercially viable.'])]
    add(4,'bm64-position-map','4.2','sl',f'{name}: interpreting positioning','Position maps',f'{name} studies the market for {sector}. The map uses average customer ratings from 1 (low) to 10 (high) for perceived price and quality. A, B and C are existing competing offers; New is the proposed offer. Ratings are perceptions, not measured technical performance or actual prices.',items,svg=svgwrap(body,760,320),check=f'Points={points}; New price betweenAandB; New quality nearA aboveB. Axes explicitly perceptions; no inference of segment size from unoccupied space.')
af(4,'bm65-niche-mass','4.2','sl','Niche and mass markets','niche market','A relatively small and specialized part of a wider market / whose customers share particular needs that a focused offer aims to meet.','Explain one advantage and one disadvantage of serving the stated niche at {name}.',[
('Gya Wear','Gya makes clothing with sensory-friendly seams for a small customer group. Customers value careful design, but specialist fabric orders are too small to qualify for bulk discounts.', ['Specialization can create differentiation.','Sensory-friendly seams address a need standard clothing may overlook.','Gya may build loyalty among customers who struggle to find alternatives.'],['Small scale can increase unit cost.','Limited fabric orders do not qualify for bulk discounts.','Gya may need a higher price to cover costs, restricting the size of the niche it can serve.']),
('Hya Tours','Hya provides quiet photography trips for wheelchair users. Customers value detailed access planning, but specialist demand is concentrated in a short season.', ['Focused expertise can create loyalty.','Detailed accessibility planning reduces uncertainty for the target travellers.','Hya can distinguish itself from generic photography tours.'],['Narrow seasonal demand creates dependence.','Few bookings outside the specialist season must cover year-round planning costs.','Hya may face unstable income even with satisfied customers.']),
('Iya Tools','Iya makes left-handed woodworking tools. Users appreciate the design, but large retailers offer little shelf space because total sales volume is low.', ['Meeting a neglected need can reduce direct competition.','Left-handed users may prefer Iya’s tools to standard designs.','A specialist reputation can support repeat recommendations.'],['Distribution can be difficult.','Retailers allocate little shelf space to low-volume products.','Iya may need direct online selling and bear the associated fulfilment cost.']),
('Jya Foods','Jya makes meals for people needing carefully controlled textures. Buyers trust the specifications, but one hospital accounts for 55% of sales.', ['Expert specifications support differentiation.','The niche values reliable texture control more than a generic low price.','Jya may build strong relationships through meeting that precise need.'],['Customer concentration creates risk.','The hospital supplies over half of sales.','Losing that contract would cause a large revenue fall even if the wider niche remained satisfied.'])])
ef(4,'bm66-research-choice','4.4','sl','Choosing market research','primary market research','Collecting new data directly for the specific research objective / through methods such as surveys, interviews, observation or focus groups.','using the proposed primary research before launch',[
('Kya Foods','Kya plans a plant-based dessert. It proposes tasting interviews with 30 current café customers, while the intended market includes supermarket buyers who rarely visit cafés.','Direct tasting can reveal specific reactions;Kya can improve flavour and texture before committing to production','The sample may be unrepresentative;current café users may differ from the intended supermarket buyers','Use tasting interviews for product refinement and supplement them with representative supermarket research before estimating demand.','The target market’s size, willingness to pay and sample profile are not known.'),
('Lya Games','Lya proposes a focus group of eight teenagers to design a family game. Adults usually pay, and dominant participants may influence quieter group members.','Discussion can reveal detailed reactions;Lya can explore why teenagers enjoy or reject a game feature','Group influence and missing buyers weaken inference;teenage opinions may not reflect adult purchasers or quieter users','Use moderated groups for ideas and separately test with family buyers and broader samples before forecasting sales.','No evidence shows how adult purchasing priorities differ from teenage preferences.'),
('Mya Retail','Mya proposes observing shoppers’ movement in a trial store. It wants to understand a display’s effect but cannot tell from observation why customers leave without buying.','Observation records actual behaviour;Mya can see whether shoppers stop at the display rather than rely on claimed interest','Reasons remain unclear;walking past a display does not reveal whether price, visibility or product fit caused rejection','Combine observation with short voluntary interviews and a controlled display trial before a chain-wide rollout.','No comparable control period or explanation of non-purchase is available.'),
('Nya Travel','Nya proposes an online survey about overnight rail trips. Respondents are recruited from its social-media followers and the survey describes the trips as exciting and sustainable.','A survey can gather structured preferences efficiently;Nya can compare desired departure dates across many respondents','Self-selection and leading language can bias results;followers attracted by sustainability may overstate general demand','Rewrite neutral questions and recruit beyond followers; use bookings or deposits to validate demand estimates.','The response rate and demographic match to actual paying travellers are unknown.')])
af(4,'bm67-sampling','4.4','sl','Sampling methods','random sampling','Selecting units from a defined population using a chance procedure / so each member has a known, usually equal, chance of selection.','Explain one strength and one weakness of the stated sampling approach at {name}.',[
('Oya Sports','Oya surveys the first 100 people leaving its own gym on a weekday morning to estimate demand for a new city-wide gym.', ['Convenience sampling is quick and relatively inexpensive.','Current visitors are readily accessible after exercise.','Oya can obtain rapid initial feedback on proposed facilities.'],['The sample is unlikely to represent the city market.','Existing weekday gym users exclude non-users and many working residents.','Demand estimates based on this group may overstate or misrepresent city-wide interest.']),
('Pya Books','Pya sets equal quotas for readers aged under 25, 25–49 and 50 or more, even though the target population is not equally divided across those groups.', ['Quota sampling ensures the selected age groups are included.','Pya can obtain some views from each group rather than missing one by chance.','This can reveal differences in reading preferences.'],['The quotas do not match the population proportions.','Equal group sizes do not produce a directly representative total.','Pya should not treat an unweighted overall percentage as the market’s true preference.']),
('Qya Foods','Qya uses a random-number generator to select 200 entries from its complete customer list. It wants to understand current customer satisfaction.', ['The chance procedure reduces interviewer selection bias.','Every listed customer has an equal chance of being chosen.','Qya can obtain more defensible evidence about existing customers than by selecting favorites.'],['Non-response can still create bias.','Selected dissatisfied or busy customers may be less likely to reply.','Random selection alone does not guarantee that actual respondents represent the list.']),
('Rya Apps','Rya randomly selects 300 paying users to research why people cancel free trials. People who cancelled are not on the paying-user list.', ['Selection within the paying-user list is unbiased by the interviewer.','The random procedure gives those listed users a chance to participate.','It can produce useful evidence about retained paying users.'],['The sampling frame excludes the relevant group.','Cancelled trial users cannot be selected.','Rya cannot reliably explain cancellation from a sample containing only those who stayed.'])])
ef(4,'bm68-life-cycle-extension','4.5','sl','Product life-cycle extension','extension strategy','A marketing action intended to prolong a product’s commercially useful life / by sustaining or renewing demand rather than immediately withdrawing it.','using the proposed extension strategy',[
('Sya Juice','Sya’s mature juice brand has stable awareness but falling sales. It proposes smaller recyclable packs for commuters; existing buyers like the current family size.','A new format can reach an additional usage occasion;small packs may appeal to commuters without changing the juice recipe','New packaging raises cost and may cannibalize existing sales;family buyers may simply switch to lower-volume purchases','Pilot the commuter pack in suitable channels while keeping family packs; expand if incremental margin exceeds packaging and cannibalization costs.','There is no evidence that commuter purchases are additional rather than substitutions.'),
('Tya Audio','Tya’s portable speaker has declining sales. It proposes a repair and battery-replacement service instead of replacing the design. Customers complain mainly about battery life.','Repair can restore value and extend customer relationships;replacing batteries addresses the stated cause of dissatisfaction','Longer product life may delay replacement sales;service income must cover skilled labour and spare parts','Offer a priced repair service if its lifetime margin and retention benefit justify fewer immediate replacements.','The repair cost, take-up and effect on future hardware purchases are unknown.'),
('Uya Games','Uya’s board game is mature. It proposes an expansion pack for existing owners, but stores report that many original games remain unopened after purchase.','An expansion can renew interest at lower development cost;Uya can use an established brand and customer base','The active audience may be smaller than sales figures suggest;unopened games are unlikely to create repeat expansion demand','Test engagement among actual players and produce a limited run before committing to wide distribution.','The number of active players and willingness to buy an expansion are unmeasured.'),
('Vya Wear','Vya’s waterproof jacket sales are falling. It proposes a new colour range and social campaign, while customer reviews mention failing zips rather than outdated colours.','A refreshed range can attract attention;new colours and promotion may stimulate interest in a familiar product','The strategy may miss the cause of decline;zip failures can damage trust even if promotion raises awareness','Fix the quality problem before promoting the refresh; extend the product only if customer evidence supports remaining demand.','The relative importance of design fatigue and defects has not been measured.')])
for name,sector,rows in [
('Wya Mobility','mobility devices',[('Swift',1.6,14,32000),('Trail',0.7,16,-12000),('Urban',1.8,2,56000),('Mini',0.4,1,-4000)]),
('Xya Foods','snacks',[('Crunch',1.4,12,24000),('Bite',0.6,15,-9000),('Classic',2.2,3,62000),('Light',0.3,1,-7000)]),
('Yya Apps','learning apps',[('Speak',1.8,18,42000),('Write',0.5,20,-18000),('Read',2.4,2,80000),('Count',0.7,3,2000)]),
('Zya Tools','garden tools',[('Cut',1.3,11,21000),('Grow',0.8,13,-6000),('Dig',1.9,1,49000),('Trim',0.4,2,-3000)])]:
    first=rows[0][0]; dog=rows[3][0];cashcow=rows[2][0]
    table='\n\n| Product | Relative market share | Market growth, % | Annual net operating cash flow, USD |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n'+'\n'.join(f'| {a} | {b:.1f} | {c} | {d:,} |' for a,b,c,d in rows)+'\n\n'
    body='<rect x="80" y="35" width="600" height="230" fill="#eff6ff" stroke="#172554"/><path d="M380 35 L380 265 M80 150 L680 150" fill="none" stroke="#172554"/>'+svg_text(160,25,'High relative share')+svg_text(460,25,'Low relative share')+svg_text(5,75,'High',13)+svg_text(5,95,'growth',13)+svg_text(5,215,'Low',13)+svg_text(5,235,'growth',13)
    items=[explain('a',f'Classify {first} and {cashcow} using the BCG matrix.',[f'{first} is a star: high relative share and high market growth.',f'{cashcow} is a cash cow: high relative share and low market growth.']),explain('b',f'Explain one reason to retain and one reason to withdraw {dog}.',[f'Retention could preserve a specialist customer relationship or a future extension opportunity for {dog}.','Low market growth and share do not prove that all customers or strategic links have disappeared.',f'Withdrawal may free resources from {dog} for stronger products.','Its weak relative position and low growth provide limited evidence of future scale; cash-flow and strategic data must also be considered.']),ten('c',f'Using the BCG matrix and other evidence, recommend which product strategy {name} should prioritize.',name,'allocating resources across the product portfolio',f'{cashcow} generates USD {rows[2][3]:,} annual operating cash and may help fund development of growth products',f'BCG categories alone omit margins, cross-selling and future change; {dog} has annual operating cash flow USD {rows[3][3]:,}',f'Protect the cash-generating established product while selectively testing growth opportunities; withdrawal or a limited extension for {dog} needs evidence on its strategic role.','No investment requirements, profit figures, customer overlap or reliability ranges for growth forecasts are supplied.')]
    add(4,'bm69-bcg-portfolio','4.5','sl',f'{name}: portfolio strategy','Product portfolio and BCG matrix',f'{name} sells {sector}. Relative market share equals the product’s share divided by its largest competitor’s share. Treat relative share of 1 or more as high and annual market growth above 10% as high. The blank matrix is provided for reference.'+table+'All cash figures are for the same year. Cash flow is not identical to accounting profit. Use the stated thresholds.',items,svg=svgwrap(body,760,300),check='Thresholds tested: rows0star,row1questionmark,row2cashcow,row3dog. Cashflow not treated as profit; matrix recommendations remain conditional on omitted strategic and investment data.')
af(4,'bm70-branding','4.5','sl','Branding','brand loyalty','A customer’s continuing preference for and repeated purchase of a particular brand / despite the availability of competing offers.','Explain two ways the stated branding issue could affect {name}.',[
('Aze Water','Aze markets refill bottles as durable and repairable. Several customers report that spare lids are unavailable; its adverts still promise lifetime repair access.', ['An unfulfilled brand promise can weaken trust.','Customers unable to obtain spare lids cannot use the promised repair option.','Repeat purchases and recommendations may fall even if the bottle itself performs well.'],['Negative reviews may deter new customers as well as existing buyers.','People researching Aze’s lifetime repair promise may find reports of unavailable lids.','Lower acquisition of first-time buyers can reduce sales even before current customers need replacement bottles.']),
('Bze Foods','Bze’s established snack brand emphasizes local ingredients. It switches to imports without changing the packaging; loyal customers discover the change through suppliers.', ['Perceived inconsistency can reduce loyalty.','Customers chose Bze partly for its local sourcing promise.','Discovering imported ingredients may make them question the reason to keep buying.'],['The premium price may become harder to sustain.','Imported ingredients remove the local-origin benefit that supported Bze’s differentiation.','If buyers demand a lower price while other costs remain unchanged, profit per snack falls.']),
('Cze Repair','Cze is known for reliable same-day phone repairs. Expansion increases average turnaround to four days while the same-day slogan remains on every shop.', ['Service failure can weaken brand differentiation.','The named speed benefit no longer matches actual turnaround.','Customers needing immediate access may choose another repairer.'],['Restoring the service promise may raise operating costs.','Cze may need extra trained repairers or overtime to reduce the four-day backlog to same-day delivery.','Those corrective costs can reduce profit even if the business retains its customers.']),
('Dze Wear','Dze’s premium clothing uses distinctive designs. A cheaper range carries the same logo but has visibly poorer stitching, and customers cannot easily distinguish the ranges.', ['Brand extension can create confusion.','The same logo links the cheaper stitching to the established premium offer.','Customers may reduce their willingness to pay for Dze’s original range.'],['The cheaper range may cannibalize premium sales.','The shared logo and unclear range distinction can encourage existing buyers to trade down.','Unless the additional volume compensates, switching purchases can reduce total contribution from the original premium range.'])])
for name,product,cost,markup,forecast,lower,lowvolume in [('Eze Cups','reusable cups',8,50,6000,10.5,8200),('Fze Lamps','desk lamps',24,40,2400,30,3200),('Gze Bags','day bags',18,60,3500,25,4800),('Hze Kits','learning kits',32,25,1800,36,2300)]:
    p=cost*(1+markup/100);rev=p*forecast;lowrev=lower*lowvolume
    items=[calc('a','Calculate the proposed cost-plus price.',f'{cost}×(1+{markup}/100).',f'USD {p:.2f} per unit.'),calc('b','Calculate forecast revenue at the cost-plus price.',f'{p:.2f}×{forecast}.',f'USD {rev:,.2f}.'),ten('c',f'Recommend whether {name} should use cost-plus pricing rather than the lower tested price.',name,'the cost-plus price',f'The cost-plus method yields USD {p:.2f} per unit and forecast revenue USD {rev:,.2f}',f'It does not directly reflect demand; the lower USD {lower:.2f} test price is forecast to sell {lowvolume:,} units and generate revenue USD {lowrev:,.2f}', 'Use the price that best supports the target market and viable profit, after verifying costs and demand; revenue alone is insufficient and cost-plus is a guide rather than proof of the best price.','The cost estimate may change with volume, forecasts are uncertain and competitors’ reactions are unknown.')]
    add(4,'bm71-cost-plus','4.5','sl',f'{name}: cost-plus pricing','Pricing methods',f'{name} sells {product}. Estimated full cost is USD {cost} per unit at the planned volume. Management proposes a {markup}% mark-up on cost, not on selling price. At that price forecast sales are {forecast:,} units. A separate market test predicts {lowvolume:,} units at USD {lower:.2f}. These are sales for the same quarter. Do not assume the full unit cost remains unchanged at a different volume. Show workings; money to two decimals.',items,kind='data-response',check=f'Costplus={p}; revenue={rev}; alternateRevenue={lowrev}; markup based oncost, no confusion with profitmargin; no inference of optimalprofit fromrevenue.')
ef(4,'bm72-penetration-premium','4.5','sl','Penetration pricing','penetration pricing','Setting a relatively low initial price to attract customers and gain market entry / with the intention of building sales or acceptance rather than maximizing initial margin.','using a low introductory penetration price',[
('Ize Transit','Ize launches an app for booking local buses. A low first-month fee may attract users, but operators charge a fixed fee per booking and users can switch apps easily.','A low price can encourage trial;users may test Ize before developing a booking habit','Low margin and weak switching barriers create risk;users may leave when the introductory fee rises','Use a time-limited trial only with a credible retention and sustainable pricing plan; track repeat use before subsidizing expansion.','The retention rate and cost per retained customer are unknown.'),
('Jze Meals','Jze launches prepared lunches at a low introductory price. Its kitchen is small, ingredients are premium and existing customers expect consistent quality.','An introductory offer can build awareness;new customers may try Jze’s meals instead of established alternatives','Demand may exceed capacity while margin is low;quality could suffer just when new users form their first impression','Limit the trial to manageable volumes and state the normal price clearly; do not buy growth at the expense of quality.','The kitchen’s spare capacity and repeat purchase rate are unmeasured.'),
('Kze Filters','Kze enters a water-filter market with a low hardware price and expects replacement-cartridge income. Some buyers use unofficial cartridges, and product safety is central to trust.','A low entry price can reduce adoption barriers;customers can try Kze’s filter without a large initial outlay','Future cartridge income is uncertain;unofficial replacements may reduce repeat revenue and harm the brand if performance is poor','Use the low price only if safe recurring revenue and affordability are credible; test cartridge retention before large subsidies.','The share of users buying official cartridges and their lifetime margin are unknown.'),
('Lze Classes','Lze offers low-price introductory language classes. Tutors are paid per session and classes need a minimum group size; nearby competitors often match discounts.','Low prices can attract initial enrolments;larger groups may spread tutor cost across more learners','Competitor matching may erase differentiation;Lze could remain at low margins without building loyal enrolment','Run a limited cohort trial emphasizing teaching quality and clear progression; stop discounting if it does not produce sustainable repeat enrolment.','Competitor reactions and conversion from discounted to standard courses are unknown.')])
ef(4,'bm73-social-promotion','4.5','sl','Promotion strategy','through-the-line promotion','An integrated promotional approach combining broad-reach and more targeted activities / to connect awareness with specific customer engagement or response.','using the proposed social-media-led promotion',[
('Mze Parks','Mze combines a city poster campaign with targeted social-media booking links for a new climbing venue. Influencers attract attention, but many followers live outside the city.','Integrated promotion can connect awareness to booking;local viewers can move from posters to a measurable online response','Follower totals may mislead;out-of-city attention may produce few paid visits to Mze’s fixed location','Target local reachable segments and measure paid bookings rather than follower counts; use influencers whose audience can attend.','No evidence links current views to incremental local bookings.'),
('Nze Foods','Nze plans recipe videos and supermarket displays for a new sauce. A paid creator refuses to label sponsored posts, arguing disclosure reduces engagement.','Videos can demonstrate product use;linked supermarket displays make it easier for viewers to find Nze’s sauce','Undisclosed sponsorship creates an ethical trust risk;customers may feel misled when they discover the paid relationship','Require transparent sponsorship and test sales uplift in stocked stores; reject promotion that relies on misleading viewers.','The campaign’s effect on repeat purchases and retailer availability is unknown.'),
('Oze Repair','Oze promotes repair subscriptions through short videos and targeted email. Customers have posted complaints about slow appointments that the team has not answered.','Targeted promotion can explain an unfamiliar service;videos show how subscription repairs work','Promotion may amplify unresolved service weaknesses;more enquiries could worsen delays and public complaints','Resolve scheduling problems and respond honestly before scaling promotion; measure service capacity alongside conversion.','The backlog and capacity available for new subscriptions are not stated.'),
('Pze Wear','Pze uses fashion influencers and direct-message discounts. It claims recycled fabric but has incomplete supplier evidence, while younger customers actively question sustainability claims.','Social media allows interaction with target buyers;Pze can explain designs and answer customer questions','Unverified sustainability claims can damage credibility;interactive channels make unsupported claims easier to challenge publicly','Verify sourcing and use precise evidence-based claims before promotion; emphasize proven benefits while evidence is incomplete.','The recycled-content evidence and willingness to pay for verified sourcing are missing.')])
ef(4,'bm74-service-seven-ps','4.5','sl','People, processes and physical evidence','physical evidence','The tangible cues that help customers judge an otherwise intangible service / such as the premises, equipment, appearance and visible service records.','redesigning the proposed service marketing mix',[
('Qze Clinics','Qze proposes attractive waiting rooms and online check-in. Patients complain more about unanswered questions than décor, and some older users cannot complete digital forms.','Better physical evidence and processes may improve confidence;clear premises and shorter queues can reassure patients','The mix may neglect people and accessibility;unanswered questions and difficult forms can still undermine the service','Train staff to explain care and retain assisted check-in alongside suitable premises improvements; measure patient understanding, not only queue time.','No evidence separates the causes of dissatisfaction or measures digital access.'),
('Rze Hotels','Rze proposes self-service arrival kiosks and premium lobby furniture. Guests value friendly local recommendations; staff would be reduced after the redesign.','A smoother process may reduce waiting;guests arriving at busy times can check in quickly','Reducing people may weaken differentiation;local advice and warmth are part of the service guests value','Use kiosks for routine arrival while retaining knowledgeable visible hosts; invest in physical cues consistent with the actual service.','The share of guests preferring self-service and the staffing savings are unknown.'),
('Sze Advice','Sze proposes an expensive office to signal professional quality. Most clients meet online and complain that advisers miss promised follow-up dates.','A professional environment can support confidence;some clients may interpret credible premises as evidence of stability','The key process failure remains;an office does not ensure advisers follow up reliably','Fix ownership and timing of follow-up first, then choose proportionate physical evidence for the largely online service.','The influence of office appearance on clients’ purchase decisions is unmeasured.'),
('Tze Fitness','Tze proposes a glossy app and redesigned reception. Instructors vary in skill and new members are unsure how to use equipment safely.','Visible quality and clearer booking can reduce uncertainty;the app may make class access easier','Weak people performance can outweigh physical cues;members still need competent instruction and safe guidance','Standardize instructor support and induction before expanding app promotion; align processes and visible evidence with real service quality.','Instructor competence and the cost of training have not been assessed.')])

for name,sector,a,b,x,history in [('Uze Travel','city tours',80,6,30,'advertising exposure has previously ranged from 5 to 20 units'),('Vze Foods','meal boxes',120,8,25,'past trials used advertising exposure from 4 to 16 units'),('Wze Books','online books',60,5,24,'the sample covered exposure values from 3 to 15 units'),('Xze Wear','sportswear',150,12,22,'historical exposure ranged from 2 to 14 units')]:
    forecast=a+b*x
    points=[(3,a+b*3-3),(7,a+b*7+4),(11,a+b*11-2),(15,a+b*15+1)]
    body='<path d="M70 260 L700 260 M70 260 L70 30" fill="none" stroke="#172554"/>'+svg_text(280,295,'Advertising exposure index, x')+svg_text(10,25,'Weekly unit sales, y',13)
    ymax=max(y for xx,y in points)+30
    for xx,yy in points:body+=f'<circle cx="{70+xx*35}" cy="{260-yy/ymax*210:.2f}" r="5" fill="#2563eb"/>'
    items=[calc('a',f'Use the fitted regression equation to forecast sales at x={x}.',f'y={a}+{b}×{x}.',f'{forecast} units per week.'),explain('b','Explain two limitations of using this forecast as a guaranteed sales target.',['The chosen exposure lies outside the historical sample range.','The prediction is extrapolation, where the fitted relationship may not continue.','The business should not treat a mathematically calculated value as validated demand.','Correlation does not establish advertising as the sole cause.','Prices, competitors, seasonality or stock availability may affect weekly sales.','A target based only on the equation may punish employees for factors outside their control.']),ten('c',f'Recommend whether {name} should expand marketing activity to the proposed exposure level.',name,'the proposed marketing expansion',f'The fitted line predicts {forecast} weekly units, suggesting a potentially useful demand response',f'The proposal extrapolates beyond the observed range and extra sales may not cover marketing cost','Use a staged controlled trial and measure incremental margin before a full increase; maintain a fallback if the relationship weakens.','The regression’s reliability, marketing cost, margins and other demand influences are not supplied.')]
    add(4,'bm75-regression-forecast','4.3','ahl',f'{name}: regression and extrapolation','Sales forecasting and regression',f'{name} sells {sector}. An analyst fits y={a}+{b}x, where y is weekly unit sales and x is an advertising-exposure index. In this case, {history}. Management proposes x={x}. The plotted observations illustrate the historical association, not all observations used to fit the equation. Use the supplied equation; do not refit from the diagram. Show workings.',items,kind='data-response',svg=svgwrap(body,760,320),check=f'Suppliedline y={a}+{b}x at{x} gives{forecast}; x outside historicalrange; not a causal guarantee. No regression refit inferred from illustrativepoints.')
for name,product,p1,q1,p2,q2 in [('Yze Films','tickets',12,5000,15,4200),('Zze Meals','meal boxes',20,3000,22,2550),('Azi Games','game licences',40,2400,36,2760),('Bzi Travel','day passes',18,6000,21,5400)]:
    dq=(q2-q1)/q1*100;dp=(p2-p1)/p1*100;ped=dq/dp;r1=p1*q1;r2=p2*q2
    items=[calc('a','Calculate PED using percentage changes from the original values, not the midpoint method.',f'Quantity change={dq:.6f}%; price change={dp:.6f}%; PED=quantity%/price%.',f'{ped:.2f}; magnitude {abs(ped):.2f}, '+('elastic' if abs(ped)>1 else 'inelastic' if abs(ped)<1 else 'unit elastic')+'.'),calc('b','Calculate the change in total revenue.',f'New revenue {p2}×{q2} minus original {p1}×{q1}.',f'USD {abs(r2-r1):,.2f} '+('increase' if r2>=r1 else 'decrease')+' in total revenue.'),ten('c',f'Recommend whether {name} should maintain the new price.',name,'the new price',f'Revenue changes from USD {r1:,} to USD {r2:,}; the observed PED magnitude is {abs(ped):.2f}',f'The observation may reflect other demand changes and revenue is not profit; unit costs and capacity affect the decision','Maintain the new price only if its incremental profit and positioning are preferable; test the response while controlling for other demand factors.','The case supplies no cost data, competitor reactions or evidence that price alone caused the quantity change.')]
    add(4,'bm76-ped','4.5','ahl',f'{name}: price elasticity','Price elasticity of demand',f'{name} sells {product}. Across two comparable weeks, price changes from USD {p1} to USD {p2} and quantity sold from {q1:,} to {q2:,}. Calculate PED using percentage changes based on the original price and quantity. Assume no other changes only for the numerical PED calculation; assess that assumption when making a recommendation. Show workings; PED and money to two decimals.',items,kind='data-response',check=f'dQ%={dq};dP%={dp};PED={ped};revenues={r1},{r2};signedchange={r2-r1}. Elasticity magnitude distinguished from negative sign; no profit inference.')
ef(4,'bm77-dynamic-price','4.5','ahl','Dynamic pricing','dynamic pricing','Changing prices in response to demand, capacity, timing or other market conditions / rather than keeping one fixed price for all transactions.','introducing the proposed dynamic pricing system',[
('Czi Rooms','Czi plans software that raises room prices when few rooms remain. Regular guests value predictable prices, and a nearby festival creates a short demand peak.','Prices can reflect scarce capacity;festival demand may generate higher revenue from Czi’s fixed room stock','Perceived unfairness can damage loyalty;regular guests may resent unexpected price increases','Use clear advance price information and sensible limits, with loyalty arrangements where justified; monitor repeat bookings after the festival.','The price sensitivity of regular and festival guests and the effect on retention are unknown.'),
('Dzi Energy','Dzi proposes time-varying prices for shared charging bays. Users can shift some charging to quieter hours, but essential users have little scheduling flexibility.','Prices can shift demand toward spare capacity;quieter-hour incentives may reduce queues and improve bay use','Inflexible users may bear higher costs;essential charging needs can make the system appear unfair','Use transparent off-peak incentives with protected essential access or capped charges; assess both utilization and user impact.','The proportion of users able to shift charging times is unmeasured.'),
('Ezi Tickets','Ezi proposes prices that rise as theatre seats sell. It wants to support affordable access, but online resellers already buy large blocks of low-price tickets.','Early pricing can build bookings and later capture high demand;fixed seats can generate more revenue when demand becomes clear','Resellers may capture the benefit instead of target customers;rapid buying could make low-price access largely unavailable','Combine transparent price bands with purchase limits and reserved access allocations before adopting broad dynamic pricing.','The prevalence of resale and customers’ willingness to accept variable prices are uncertain.'),
('Fzi Delivery','Fzi would add higher delivery fees during rain and peak hours. Drivers face harder work, but customers in remote areas have few alternatives.','Higher fees can encourage driver supply when demand is high;extra reward may support peak-hour availability','High fees can harm trust and access;remote customers may face little choice during bad weather','Use disclosed caps and ensure some fee supports driver availability; assess whether reliability improves enough to justify the charge.','The relationship between added fees, driver supply and actual waiting times is unknown.')])
ef(4,'bm78-international-market','4.6','ahl','International marketing','international marketing','Planning and implementing marketing activities across national borders / while responding to differences in customers and the external business environment.','adapting the marketing mix for the proposed overseas market',[
('Gzi Foods','Gzi plans to sell its snack in another country. Research suggests different flavour preferences and package sizes, but separate production and promotion would cost more than one standard offer.','Adaptation can improve local relevance;flavours and sizes can match the researched overseas preferences','Separate variants reduce economies and coordination simplicity;Gzi may incur higher production and promotional costs','Adapt the most important attributes supported by research while standardizing elements customers accept; pilot before national distribution.','The sample’s representativeness and the extra margin from adaptation are unknown.'),
('Hzi Apps','Hzi enters a market where many users share devices and pay through local mobile wallets. Its current product assumes personal devices and card subscriptions.','Process adaptation removes access barriers;shared-device features and local payments can make Hzi usable by target customers','Development and support costs rise;local payment integration and privacy on shared devices require additional work','Prioritize secure local access and test willingness to pay; retain common core content where possible to control cost.','The number of viable paying users and local integration costs are not known.'),
('Izi Wear','Izi wants to use the same adverts overseas. Local interviews suggest different expectations about modesty and family purchasing roles; managers caution against treating every consumer as culturally identical.','Local adaptation can avoid inappropriate messages;research can improve relevance to actual purchasing roles','Overgeneralization can misdirect the campaign;national stereotypes may ignore diverse segments within the country','Use segment research and local testing, adapting messages where evidence supports it rather than assuming one cultural profile.','The interviews’ coverage and differences within the market are unclear.'),
('Jzi Tools','Jzi enters an overseas market with fragmented rural retail. Direct online selling would preserve control, but delivery and after-sales repair are difficult outside cities.','Local distribution and repair partnerships may improve access;customers can obtain support where Jzi has no own network','Partner margins and control requirements increase costs;inconsistent repairs could damage the brand','Use qualified local service partners with clear standards and a limited regional launch before wider expansion.','Partner capability, rural order density and delivery costs are unverified.')])
for name,launch,durations in [('Kzi Coffee','a café launch',[2,3,2,1]),('Lzi Wear','a clothing launch',[3,2,3,1]),('Mzi Games','a board-game launch',[2,4,2,2]),('Nzi Books','a subscription launch',[1,3,2,1])]:
    a,b,c,d=durations;finish=max(a+b,a+c)+d
    schedule=[('Research',0,a),('Creative',a,b),('Channel setup',a,c),('Launch test',max(a+b,a+c),d)]
    body=''
    for j,(task,start,dur) in enumerate(schedule):
        body+=svg_text(10,55+j*50,task,14)+f'<rect x="{165+start*45}" y="{32+j*50}" width="{dur*45}" height="30" fill="#93c5fd" stroke="#172554"/>'
    for t in range(finish+1):body+=svg_text(160+t*45,265,t,12)
    body+=svg_text(290,292,'Weeks from project start')
    items=[explain('a','State which two tasks can run in parallel and the scheduled completion week.',['Creative and channel setup run in parallel after research.',f'The launch test ends at the end of week {finish}.']),explain('b','Explain how the Gantt chart can help coordinate this marketing launch.',['It shows task timing and overlap.','The team can see that creative work and channel setup both depend on research.','Resources can be scheduled before the common launch-test date.','A delayed prerequisite can be identified and the launch date reviewed rather than assuming all tasks are independent.']),explain('c','Explain one limitation of relying on this chart alone.',['The bars use estimated durations.','Creative revision or channel approval may take longer than forecast.','The chart does not ensure sufficient people or funds are available when tasks overlap.','The team must verify resources and update the schedule when evidence changes.'])]
    add(4,'bm79-gantt-launch','4.2','ahl',f'{name}: marketing launch schedule','HL Gantt-chart toolkit',f'{name} plans {launch}. Research starts at week 0; creative work and channel setup start immediately after research. The launch test starts only when both are complete. Bars show planned work and all durations are whole weeks. There are no holidays or resource constraints for the schedule calculation.',items,svg=svgwrap(body,760,320),check=f'Schedule={schedule}; completion=max({a+b},{a+c})+{d}={finish}; overlaps not summed sequentially.',scope='4.2 marketing planning integrated with explicitly HL-only toolkit Gantt charts.')
ef(4,'bm80-market-leadership','4.1','ahl','Market leadership','market leadership','Holding the largest market share in a defined market / relative to competing businesses, measured using a consistent basis such as value or volume.','prioritizing market leadership over current profit margin',[
('Ozi Fitness','Ozi can cut membership fees to overtake the largest local gym by member count. Its facilities are already crowded at peak times and profit per member would fall.','Leadership may increase visibility and bargaining power;more members can make Ozi attractive to suppliers and partners','Volume growth can weaken service and margin;crowding may cause valuable members to leave','Pursue share only if capacity and lifetime margin support it; profitable retention may be preferable to a short-lived membership lead.','The cost of extra capacity and retention response are unknown.'),
('Pzi Meals','Pzi can win a national retailer contract that would make it the volume leader. The retailer demands a low price and lengthy payment terms; existing independent shops pay faster.','Leadership can improve distribution reach;the retailer gives Pzi access to many new customers','Low margins and slow payment create financial risk;volume leadership may consume working capital while weakening return','Accept only if cash finance and sustainable contract margin are secured; do not replace reliable profitable channels solely for rank.','The full contract cost, payment reliability and working-capital requirement are missing.'),
('Qzi Apps','Qzi wants the most free users in its category. New users create server costs, and advertising income depends on active use rather than registrations.','A large user base can support network effects and awareness;leadership may attract advertisers if users remain active','Registrations may be a weak commercial measure;inactive users add little revenue while growth still uses resources','Prioritize active retention and viable monetization, using market leadership as an outcome rather than an isolated target.','No active-user, acquisition-cost or revenue-per-user forecasts are provided.'),
('Rzi Lamps','Rzi could become the largest seller by units through a basic low-price range. Its premium repairable range generates most profit and attracts environmentally conscious customers.','Higher unit share may spread overhead and improve supplier terms;the basic range can reach price-sensitive customers','A volume target can dilute premium positioning;low-price sales may cannibalize repairable lamps and reduce margin','Use distinct positioning and verify incremental profit before targeting leadership; preserve the premium proposition if it creates greater long-term value.','The extent of cannibalization and actual supplier savings are unknown.')],scope='4.1 importance of market share and market leadership is explicitly HL-only AO3 content.')
